---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Card from '../components/Card.astro';
import { withBase } from '../lib/base-path.mjs';

const sports = [
  {
    title: 'Basketball',
    description:
      'Full vocabulary today: actions, court profiles, named positions, and worked example drills.',
    status: 'Full',
  },
  {
    title: 'Soccer',
    description:
      'Reserved. A provisional vocabulary is stubbed and waiting for sport experts to shape it.',
    status: 'Reserved',
  },
  {
    title: 'Handball',
    description:
      'Reserved. Court, entities, and frames carry over; the action list is open for contribution.',
    status: 'Reserved',
  },
  {
    title: 'Hockey',
    description:
      'Reserved. The shared structure applies; a hockey-specific action set is yet to be defined.',
    status: 'Reserved',
  },
  {
    title: 'Futsal',
    description:
      'Reserved. Same document shape, awaiting a futsal vocabulary from the community.',
    status: 'Reserved',
  },
];

const jsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'SoftwareSourceCode',
  name: 'Open Coaching Format',
  description:
    'An open standard for representing sports coaching diagrams, drill animations, and playbooks as structured data.',
  codeRepository: 'https://github.com/opencoachingformat/spec',
  license: 'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/',
  programmingLanguage: 'JSON Schema',
};
---
<Base
  title="Home"
  description="An open standard for representing sports coaching diagrams, drill animations, and playbooks as structured data."
>
  <Fragment slot="head">
    <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(jsonLd)} />
  </Fragment>

  <section class="hero">
    <h1>Open Coaching Format</h1>
    <p class="claim">The only limit is your tool — not the format.</p>
    <p class="tagline">
      OCF describes what happens — the plays, the drills — never how it
      looks, so any tool can show it the way its users need.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Locked in proprietary apps</h2>
    <p>
      Coaches draw plays every day, but the diagrams live inside closed tools —
      the FIBA Europe Coaching App, FastDraw, Sportplan.net, and dozens of
      others. Each one stores plays in its own private format and lets you
      export only a flat image: a PNG or a PDF.
    </p>
    <p>
      An image can be viewed, but it cannot be understood by another program.
      You cannot search it, replay the movement, translate the labels, restyle
      it for a handout, or move a play from one app to another. The moment a
      drill leaves the app that drew it, it stops being data and becomes a
      picture.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Even digital tools kept the whiteboard</h2>
    <p>
      Coaches have sketched plays on a whiteboard or a printed sheet for
      decades — one static picture at a time. Some coaching apps have moved
      past that and can already animate a sequence. But most still organize
      a drill around the same idea underneath: discrete frames, each one its
      own still image to build.
    </p>
    <p>
      OCF started there too. Early versions grouped a drill into frames, and
      while positions were meant to carry forward from one to the next,
      nothing actually enforced that continuity. In practice, the frame
      boundary itself got in the way: sketching out a whole sequence meant
      constantly working around it, and it was easy to end up with actions
      that never showed up animated at all — because they'd landed in the
      wrong frame.
    </p>
    <p>
      Today OCF has no frames. A play is an ordered sequence of actions,
      each one following from what came before — and it can branch:
      outcomes like a make or a miss lead into different continuations, and
      a drill can even be marked to loop back to where it started. All of
      that is optional — but when you use it, the validator checks it's
      both semantically and technically sound, not just schema-valid.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>What OCF is</h2>
    <p>
      The Open Coaching Format is an open standard that stores a coaching
      diagram — or a whole animated drill — as readable JSON. JSON is a simple,
      text-based way of describing information that both people and software can
      read. Because an OCF play is data rather than an image, any tool can open
      it, render it, animate it, or convert it.
    </p>
    <p>
      The same file can drive a static diagram for a printed playbook and a
      frame-by-frame animation on screen. Nothing is locked to one vendor: the
      format is public, and anyone can build a reader, an editor, or a renderer
      on top of it.
    </p>
    <p>
      OCF is really two things developed together: the JSON format itself,
      and a validator that enforces its rules — schema shape, action
      references, ball possession, branch/continuum coherence, and more. The
      renderer, the LLM-assisted authoring tools, and other projects in the
      ecosystem are reference implementations, built to show what's possible
      on top of OCF — not the standard itself.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>One format, every sport</h2>
    <p>
      OCF is not basketball-only. A play in any team sport is the same kind of
      thing: players positioned on a marked surface, moving and passing over
      time. OCF captures that shared shape once, then lets each sport add its
      own vocabulary of actions on top.
    </p>
    <p>
      Basketball is fully specified today. The other sports are reserved:
      the structure already fits them, and their action vocabularies are open
      for the people who know each game best.
    </p>
    <div class="card-grid">
      {sports.map((sport) => (
        <Card
          title={sport.title}
          description={sport.description}
          href={withBase('/learn/')}
          status={sport.status}
        />
      ))}
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>How an OCF document is shaped</h2>
    <p>
      You do not need to read JSON to get the idea. An OCF play is built from a
      few plain parts:
    </p>
    <ul class="structure-list">
      <li>
        <strong>Court coordinates.</strong> A chosen court profile fixes the size and
        markings of the playing surface, so every position is measured in real
        units — a spot on the floor means the same thing to every tool.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Entities and actions.</strong> Players, defenders, and balls are
        the entities. What they do — pass, cut, screen, dribble — are the
        actions, drawn from a sport's vocabulary.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Triggers and branching.</strong> Each action's position follows
        from the actions before it — no manual per-step coordinates. A
        <code>trigger</code> ties an action's start to another action or event,
        and a <code>branch</code> lets a play split on an outcome like a made or
        missed shot, which is what turns a static diagram into a replayable
        sequence.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Where to go next</h2>
    <div class="card-grid">
      <Card
        title="Learn"
        description="Understand the concepts and browse the format sport by sport."
        href={withBase('/learn/')}
      />
      <Card
        title="Use"
        description="Validate and render your own OCF documents, and see the supported court profiles."
        href={withBase('/use/')}
      />
      <Card
        title="Build"
        description="The specification, JSON Schema, error codes, and the tools built on OCF."
        href={withBase('/build/')}
      />
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>

<style>
  .hero {
    margin-bottom: 2.5rem;
  }
  .claim {
    font-size: 1.5rem;
    font-weight: 700;
    color: var(--text-primary);
    margin: 0.5rem 0 0.75rem;
  }
  .tagline {
    font-size: 1.15rem;
    color: var(--text-muted);
  }
  .card-grid {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(220px, 1fr));
    gap: 1rem;
    margin-top: 1rem;
  }
  .structure-list {
    margin-top: 0.5rem;
    padding-left: 1.25rem;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 0.75rem;
  }
</style>
